# Clean Code: Design Principles

## Minimize Side Effects

Functions that modify external state are harder to test and reason about. Isolate side effects to specific locations.

```
// BAD - Hidden side effect
function calculateTotal(cart) {
    cart.lastCalculated = new Date()  // Side effect!
    return cart.items.reduce(...)
}

// GOOD - Pure calculation, explicit side effect
function calculateTotal(items) {
    return items.reduce(...)
}

function updateCartWithTotal(cart) {
    cart.total = calculateTotal(cart.items)
    cart.lastCalculated = new Date()
}
```

## Prefer Immutability

Immutable data is easier to reason about — you don't need to track where it might have changed.

```
// BAD - Mutates input
function addItem(cart, item) {
    cart.items.push(item)
    return cart
}

// GOOD - Returns new object
function addItem(cart, item) {
    return {
        ...cart,
        items: [...cart.items, item]
    }
}
```

## Law of Demeter

Don't chain through objects. Each unit should only talk to its immediate collaborators.

```
// BAD - Reaching through objects
user.getAccount().getSettings().getPreferences().getTheme()

// GOOD - Ask for what you need directly
user.getTheme()
// or
userPreferencesService.getTheme(userId)
```

## Composition Over Inheritance

Favor combining simple objects over building inheritance hierarchies.

## Make Dependencies Explicit

Inject dependencies rather than hiding them. This improves testability and makes relationships clear.

```
// BAD - Hidden dependency
class OrderService {
    processOrder(order) {
        Database.save(order)  // Hidden static dependency
        EmailService.send()   // Hidden static dependency
    }
}

// GOOD - Explicit dependencies
class OrderService {
    constructor(database, emailService) {
        this.database = database
        this.emailService = emailService
    }
    
    processOrder(order) {
        this.database.save(order)
        this.emailService.send()
    }
}
```

